01:27:40,357 --> 01:27:41,857
and there'll be others.

2232
01:27:41,890 --> 01:27:43,358
TOM:
Yeah, well, you're sleeping
on the couch, Herc.

2233
01:27:43,392 --> 01:27:45,462
- I hope we shall
withstand them.

2234
01:27:45,495 --> 01:27:49,232
Somehow the gods will be kind
if we just love one another.

2235
01:27:49,265 --> 01:27:51,533
JOEL:
Are they in heaven?

2236
01:27:51,567 --> 01:27:54,437
Now Herc, no more
business trips, okay?

2237
01:27:56,038 --> 01:27:58,573
TOM:
This is the end of
the story of forgetfulness.

2238
01:27:58,607 --> 01:28:01,577
Those who watch it
will die of boredom.

2239
01:28:01,610 --> 01:28:04,547
[Growls]

2240
01:28:04,580 --> 01:28:05,615
JOEL:
Stinky.

2241
01:28:20,397 --> 01:28:22,398
- Hey, Joelster,
why these kind of movies?

2242
01:28:22,432 --> 01:28:24,100
- Well,
that's a good question, Gypsy.

2243
01:28:24,133 --> 01:28:25,968
However,
there aren't any simple answers.

2244
01:28:26,002 --> 01:28:27,503
- Well, yes there are.

2245
01:28:27,537 --> 01:28:29,005
- I think maybe
it's a cultural thing.

2246
01:28:29,038 --> 01:28:31,273
You know, the post-war era.

2247
01:28:31,307 --> 01:28:32,875
- Right,
that's exactly right, Crow.

2248
01:28:32,908 --> 01:28:35,612
You know, Western culture
had a real need

2249
01:28:35,645 --> 01:28:38,613
during the Cold War
for fantastic spectacle.

2250
01:28:38,647 --> 01:28:39,748
- Yeah, spectacles.

2251
01:28:39,782 --> 01:28:41,050
- Yeah, it's true.

2252
01:28:41,083 --> 01:28:43,285
After Liz Taylor accepted
over a million dollars

2253
01:28:43,318 --> 01:28:45,187
for a role in Cleopatra,

2254
01:28:45,220 --> 01:28:46,422
you know,
big budget blockbusters

2255
01:28:46,455 --> 01:28:47,990
became the coin of the realm.

2256
01:28:48,023 --> 01:28:49,692
- No, wrong guys, wrong.

2257
01:28:49,725 --> 01:28:51,427
- I was thinking
more along the lines

2258
01:28:51,460 --> 01:28:53,830
of European indignation
toward post-war conservatism

2259
01:28:53,863 --> 01:28:56,265
and sexual repression
which translated

2260
01:28:56,298 --> 01:28:57,433
onto the screen

2261
01:28:57,467 --> 01:28:59,335
into big sweaty guys
pushing girls around.

2262
01:28:59,368 --> 01:29:01,037
- Crow, you are so high.

2263
01:29:01,070 --> 01:29:03,072
- No, come on,
Crow's got a point.

2264
01:29:03,105 --> 01:29:04,307
Without sex,

2265
01:29:04,340 --> 01:29:06,742
would Hercules Unchained
even been made?

2266
01:29:06,775 --> 01:29:08,410
- Joseph Campbell.

2267
01:29:08,443 --> 01:29:09,545
ALL:
Huh?

2268
01:29:09,578 --> 01:29:11,113
- Joseph Campbell?

2269
01:29:11,147 --> 01:29:12,315
- Oh.

2270
01:29:12,348 --> 01:29:14,250
What-- I think
what our bright young friend

2271
01:29:14,283 --> 01:29:17,820
is trying to remind us of
is the need for myth.

2272
01:29:17,853 --> 01:29:19,422
You know?
The collective dream.

2273
01:29:19,455 --> 01:29:20,557
- Yeah, yeah.

2274
01:29:20,590 --> 01:29:21,557
There's a need
for gods and heroes.

2275
01:29:21,590 --> 01:29:22,925
I remember
that Joseph Campbell

2276
01:29:22,958 --> 01:29:24,893
said something
in one of those books of his

2277
01:29:24,927 --> 01:29:30,165
with masks and stuff in it
and that this thing,

2278
01:29:30,198 --> 01:29:31,333
you're supposed to do--

2279
01:29:31,367 --> 01:29:32,868
- Wrong, wrong,
wrong, wrong...

2280
01:29:32,902 --> 01:29:34,803
- Come on, I think
we can probe deeper

2281
01:29:34,837 --> 01:29:37,340
into the phenomela--
phenume--

2282
01:29:37,373 --> 01:29:39,342
- Phenomenological.

2283
01:29:39,375 --> 01:29:40,777
- Yeah, I think we can
probe deeper into

2284
01:29:40,810 --> 01:29:44,647
the phenomenological basis
for Hercules' experience.

2285
01:29:44,680 --> 01:29:46,682
- Discarding
the classical dualistic view

2286
01:29:46,715 --> 01:29:47,683
of course, and--

2287
01:29:47,717 --> 01:29:49,018
- How do you figure?

2288
01:29:49,052 --> 01:29:50,487
- Oh, well, Gypsy,
every phenomenon

2289
01:29:50,520 --> 01:29:54,090
belongs to our regional ontology
by virtue of its essence.

2290
01:29:54,123 --> 01:29:55,825
- Look, I'll make it easy
for all of you!

2291
01:29:55,858 --> 01:29:57,960
Joseph E. Levine
picked these turkeys up

2292
01:29:57,993 --> 01:30:00,830
after a sagging European box
drove the price way down,

2293
01:30:00,863 --> 01:30:02,232
then he dumped them,
brought them to America,

2294
01:30:02,265 --> 01:30:05,400
did a massive distribution deal
and made a fortune, okay?

2295
01:30:05,433 --> 01:30:07,270
- You're wrong.

2296
01:30:07,303 --> 01:30:08,905
- Oh.
- Philistine.

2297
01:30:08,938 --> 01:30:11,207
- Tom.
- Well, well, it's true.

2298
01:30:11,240 --> 01:30:12,208
Am I right, sirs?

2299
01:30:12,242 --> 01:30:13,575
I mean, come on,
back me up on this.

2300
01:30:15,343 --> 01:30:16,312
- Yeah.

2301
01:30:16,345 --> 01:30:17,380
Oh, well.

2302
01:30:17,413 --> 01:30:21,117
Steve, is that right?

2303
01:30:21,150 --> 01:30:23,052
- Uh, yeah I guess so.

2304
01:30:23,085 --> 01:30:24,353
I mean, there were
these guys in suits

2305
01:30:24,387 --> 01:30:26,055
that used to drive up the coast

2306
01:30:26,088 --> 01:30:28,090
and check on us
every now and then.

2307
01:30:28,123 --> 01:30:29,792
I didn't really
get to meet them much.

2308
01:30:29,825 --> 01:30:31,427
I was usually up on the cliffs

2309
01:30:31,460 --> 01:30:33,095
drinking that wine
they got there.

2310
01:30:33,128 --> 01:30:35,330
It's really cheap,
it's about 13 cents a bottle.

2311
01:30:35,363 --> 01:30:36,965
- Thank you, Steve.

2312
01:30:36,998 --> 01:30:38,167
- Yeah, no problem.

2313
01:30:38,200 --> 01:30:39,435
- Well, 'til next time...
[indistinct]

2314
01:30:39,468 --> 01:30:41,637
Push the button, Frank.

2315
01:30:41,670 --> 01:30:42,738
- Hey, I dig your hair, man.

2316
01:30:42,772 --> 01:30:44,373
- Frank!
- Can I have your autograph?

2317
01:30:44,407 --> 01:30:45,440
- Frank!

2318
01:30:45,473 --> 01:30:47,343
- Oh, okay.

2319
01:30:47,377 --> 01:30:49,378
- Are you any relation
to Keanu Reeves?

2320
